531547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531547 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531547 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531547 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531547 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531547 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efa5e756-b09f-5eba-be5d-3837c1691e2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531555 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531555 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531555 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531555 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ff504920-6a29-5379-af64-ee43cc431a4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531559 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531559 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531559 #edb/ref 1071 "The city of Mexicali, in Mexico, is $6.6$ feet below sea level, while the city of Porto Alegre, in Brazil, is $55.8$ feet above sea level. What is the difference between the elevations of both cities?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f0c1499-18ff-5c0e-854a-a4a1f5ed7366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531559 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the difference between the elevations of both cities, we must calculate the difference between the city with the highest elevation and the city with the lowest elevation. That is, we must calculate $55.8-(-6.6).$ This gives\n\n$$\n55.8-(-6.6)=55.8+6.6=62.4\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531561 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103627" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531561 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531561 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{2x-3y+8z}{6}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15a43dbb-9c91-5be9-a586-59bfd5235547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531561 #edb/ref 1074 "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-3y+8z}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{2x}{6}-\\frac{3y}{6}+\\frac{8z}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{6}\\cdot x-\\frac{3}{6}\\cdot y+\\frac{8}{6}\\cdot z & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot y+\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot z & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{y}{2}+\\frac{4z}{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531562 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $105÷0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17babc52-289e-5d9d-bc9c-baaf511d4b6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531562 #edb/ref 1074 "We cannot divide any number by zero. Therefore, $105÷0$ is undefined." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531582 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531582 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531582 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9a/9a1f9f1877ae0ecbc37464c5b8b20821070e7927e59dea8d930ceb8f37313722.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a parallelogram?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531582 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ba51678-bfa5-5eea-a51d-29e6a8b3feb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531582 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a parallelogram is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a parallelogram since it has $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. Therefore, its opposite sides are parallel, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since the opposite sides of a rhombus are parallel, it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a kite. It has no pairs of opposite sides that are parallel, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\nTherefore, shapes $P,$ $Q,$ and $R$ are parallelograms. The shape $S$ is not parallelogram." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531586 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531586 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531586 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531586 #edb/ref 1071 "What number can be added to $-35$ to get a sum of $0?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531586 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42762342-58bc-5c22-b022-fb45988d86d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531586 #edb/ref 1074 "The number that can be added to $-35$ to give $0$ is the additive inverse of $-35.$\n\nTo find the additive inverse of the number $-35$, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $(-)$ in front of it. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n-\\,(-35)=35.\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we simply add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n(-35)+35=-35+35=0\\,✓\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531588 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531588 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531588 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531588 #edb/ref 1071 "$240÷(-12)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531588 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43993959-de6b-5c51-8ec3-a8748bbd2836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531588 #edb/ref 1074 "We have to divide a positive number $(240)$ by a negative one $(-12).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n240÷(-12)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $240$ by $12$ without any signs: $240÷12=20.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n240÷(-12)=-20.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531591 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531591 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101460" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531591 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531591 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/de/de806ae4a790c7339d49e362204a7a16257a3cb43165e0992f9a35afffed01bd.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ hexagons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531591 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b671a86-bc70-5855-bc03-8c1fbe9580d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531591 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $2$ triangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $10$ hexagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to hexagons is $2:10.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,10 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:5$ is equivalent to $2:10.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:5$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $5$ hexagons.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531593 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531593 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531593 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531593 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c9/c9b42a179625b667cb54f1b597127809d851a1b7a830a2632b09402ecb790330.png)\n\nWhat is the distance between the points $(3,2)$  and $(-2,2)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531593 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "553e8be5-17eb-5fb4-be35-fa84cdea996b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531593 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $5$ units, so the distance equals $5.$\n\n![](images/49/498674b4a4667b57be1574a081bbb948350754ed8993c14428ad8f31ee17bd90.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/f3/f3811804824e802c4cb20af85bac7f21f6c4e0f60e0430607c876e1cf27b7cd3.png)\n\nThe point $(-2,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $(3,2)$ is at a distance of $3$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $2+3=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531609 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531609 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531609 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531609 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a rational number\n\nII. $\\frac{3}{4}$ is a rational number\n\nIII. $5$ is a rational number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531609 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76ebc4d7-7b51-5dea-a66f-038cad1e5104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531609 #edb/ref 1074 "A rational number is any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a rational number. It can be written as $\\frac{(-3)}{4}.$\n\n- $\\frac{3}{4}$ is a rational number.\n\n- $5$ is a rational number. It can be written as $\\frac{5}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531631 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531631 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531631 #edb/ref 1071 "A water tank with a capacity of $5,000$ liters is filled with water to $42.5%$ of its capacity. How much water is in the tank?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531631 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aeb2c9f4-4f11-5f7d-ac23-e92ca6ecec4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531631 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the tank, which is $5,000$ liters. The percentage is $42.5%,$ and the part is the amount of water in the tank.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n42.5%=42.5÷100=0.425\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =42.5%\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =0.425\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =2,125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42.5%$ of $5,000$ is $2,125.$ Thus, there are $2,125$ liters of water in the tank.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n42.5%=\\frac{42.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =42.5%\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{42.5}{100}\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =42.5\\times \\frac{5,000}{100} \\\\  & =42.5\\times 50 \\\\  & =2,125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42.5%$ of $5,000$ is $2,125.$ Thus, there are $2,125$ liters of water in the tank." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531639 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531639 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531639 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531639 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7?$\n\nI. $523$\n\nII. $532$\n\nIII. $501$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531639 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cca0b3f3-f64a-53b6-8329-0c693549bc28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531639 #edb/ref 1074 "To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $523,$ we have that \n\n$$\n52-2(3)=52-6=46\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $523$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $532,$ we have that \n\n$$\n53-2(2)=53-4=49\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $532$ is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $501,$ we have that \n\n$$\n50-2(1)=50-2=48\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $501$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531646 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531646 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531646 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531646 #edb/ref 1071 "$(-5)(2x+3y)-5y=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531646 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dcca9682-5c6e-581c-bcc3-60c96a3dddcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531646 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we distribute the $(-5)$ over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}(-5)(2x+3y)-5y & = \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 2x+(-5)\\cdot 3y-5y & = \\\\ (-10x)+(-15y)-5y & = \\\\ -10x-15y-5y\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-10x-15y-5y & = \\\\ -10x+(-15y-5y) & = \\\\ -10x-20y\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531656 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531656 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531656 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531656 #edb/ref 1071 "$(-5)÷4$ is equivalent to" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531656 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f462c87b-3e02-54b6-b206-e6def06b7c2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531656 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we are dividing a negative number $(-5)$ by a positive one $(4).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n(-5)÷4=-\\,\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that\n\n$$\n5÷4=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n(-5)÷4=-1\\,\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531657 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531657 #edb/ref 1069 "q-155053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531657 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531657 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a3/a31a793df705b6cbe8dddb648ca6a0d2ebb6f4eb724ff8b99682f48cafb072a0.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531657 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8c7f0d6-8dc6-549e-8375-7c33cf7277e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531657 #edb/ref 1074 "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/aa/aad13ab456bc61c90522725ddb60f481c6138a9e53a50136d691ddd344349cf1.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/14/143e831321a485a0e57b706c6df89273bd92b3134d691d1ef41a67b7f7076481.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/2c/2c361eea2a598de063dc62375716ca5bdb7b0a06d4c30cf9a5c420cd9cc48e4b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531666 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531666 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "149d1a19-2ffd-5641-9b81-20469dfa7fad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531666 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531668 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531668 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1dbe9819-43f5-518d-a9b8-11c1b1c0c27e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531668 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531670 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531670 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2056eab2-38f7-5976-8f09-20068e76705f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531670 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531671 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531671 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21f9a1a7-88ff-59a0-8da2-849307e11017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531671 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531672 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531672 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "223c7009-a395-59ef-a320-3fe78d6b6feb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531672 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531673 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531673 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22dfee4b-7e7f-5769-a8f0-3d5f4212581e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531673 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186106387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531675 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531675 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2737bee5-3db5-532d-8e68-2d35e062cc87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531675 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531646 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
